{{Short description|Legal and social situation of transgender people in Balisca}}{{Life in Balisca}}
'''[[W:Transgender rights|Transgender rights]]''' in [[Balisca]] are among the most comprehensive in the world, reflecting the [[Accelerationism|accelerationist]] commitment to [[W:Deconstruction|radical deconstruction]] and [[W:Gender equality|gender equality]] following the [[Baliscan Revolution]] of 1919. The Confederation guarantees constitutional protections against discrimination based on [[W:Gender identity|gender identity]], provides universal access to [[W:Transgender health care|transgender health care]] through the [[Healthcare in Balisca|universal healthcare system]], and recognizes [[W:Gender non-binary|non-binary]] and diverse gender identities in official documentation. The relatively permissiveness of the post-Revolution period allowed for radical reimagining of gender relations, leading to an explosion in pioneering research and treatment for various matters regarding [[W:Gender|gender]] and [[W:Sexuality|sexuality]], including [[W:Gay|gay]], [[W:Transgender|transgender]], and [[W:Intersex|intersex]] topics.

Indigenous perspectives on gender diversity have influenced contemporary Baliscan understandings of [[W:Gender|gender as a social construct]], particularly following the indigenous decolonization and [[W:Post-gender|post-gender]] movements of the 1970s and 1980s. The [[Wallche people|Wallche]] concept of the [[weye|''weye'']]—individuals who embody both masculine and feminine spiritual characteristics—and similar roles in [[Kuarupíba|Kuarupí]] and [[Kurupiranga]] traditions provided indigenous Baliscans with cultural frameworks for understanding [[W:Gender diversity|gender diversity]] that predated Illypnian colonialism. The prevalence of these traditions prompted the [[Secretariat of Indigenous Affairs]] to formally acknowledge [[W:Gender nonconformity|gender nonconformity]], [[W:Third gender|third gender]] and other indigenous gender-diverse identities in 1977, permitting registration of non-binary gender markers on official documentation, which became official with the adoption of the [[Basic Law: Family, Personhood and Community]] (1978). 

This recognition has created spaces for cultural resurgence around traditional gender roles, with most indigenous communities reviving ceremonial roles for ''weye'' individuals that had been suppressed during the colonial period. The intersection of indigenous gender traditions with [[W:Laicite|revolutionary secularism]] has produced new cultural formations.

